# gpao's own -start-height flag decides whether one RPC call at startup is
# fatal or just a retried annoyance. With -start-height 0 (the default), run()
# queries the node for its tip to decide where to begin, and a failure there
# is fatal: the query happens before the verifier goroutine even starts, so
# nothing is left running to retry it (oracle.go:265-269). The identical
# Status() call inside the polling loop, reached once -start-height >= 1
# skips the startup query, is not fatal -- a failure there is logged and the
# loop just ticks again (oracle.go:303-306). Same dead endpoint, same RPC
# call, two different outcomes depending only on one flag.
#
# newOracle makes no network call of its own, and queryBlockMaxGas
# (oracle.go:258) falls back gracefully on failure, so nothing fails earlier
# than the query above -- a supervisor that starts gpao before the chain's
# RPC is listening hits exactly this on every boot.
#
# 127.0.0.1:1 is a privileged port: FindFreePort's ephemeral bind
# (net.Listen("tcp", "127.0.0.1:0")) never gets handed a port below 1024, so
# this address can never collide with one the cluster or gpao itself picked
# dynamically. Nothing listens there, so a connect to it is refused within
# milliseconds -- deterministic, unlike a blackholed address, which would make
# arm 1 depend on how long a dropped packet takes to time out.
#
# Neither arm below ever reaches $RPC_ADDR: both name this dead endpoint
# instead. That is why the scenario can live in this shared suite rather than
# needing a cluster of its own -- it disturbs nothing the scenarios around it
# depend on.

# Arm 1: the fatal path. eventually refuses negation, so this cannot be
# eventually-wrapped -- the failure has to be deterministic on the first
# attempt, which a refused connect is.
! gpao start -start-height 0 -remote tcp://127.0.0.1:1
stderr 'failed to query node status'

# serveStatus's own log line (oracle.go:243), written before the fatal query
# ever runs (oracle.go:260-261 precedes :265-269) -- proof that the operator
# sees the real captured process output, not just the harness's own wrapped
# error text.
stderr 'gpao: status API on'

# Arm 2: the survivable path, same dead endpoint. Skipping the startup query
# is what gets Start to return at all -- by then the polling loop has not yet
# ticked once, so nothing has exercised its tolerance. Surviving PAST the
# first tick is the separate thing, and that is oracle.go:303-306: the same
# Status() call fails there too, and is only logged before the loop ticks
# again.
gpao start -start-height 1 -remote tcp://127.0.0.1:1

# Two seconds, not one: gpao's default -poll-interval is exactly 1s
# (main.go:40), so sleeping one would land on the first tick's boundary and
# leave it a coin flip whether the in-loop failure this arm is about had
# happened yet. Two guarantees at least one refused tick has been logged and
# survived.
#
# daemon.Start's settle window is a weaker guarantee than it looks: it starts
# when the READY PROBE first succeeds, not at launch (daemon.go:235-244), so
# it proves only that the process outlived that window. This is what proves it
# kept running afterwards, still polling a dead endpoint. A dead process would
# fail here the way arm 1 failed -- $GPAO_STATUS would refuse the connection
# rather than answer 200.
sleep 2s

# Answering at all is the claim: a gpao that had exited would refuse this
# connection outright, on this attempt and on every later one, so retrying
# cannot turn a dead process into a live one. What the retry does cover is a
# connect to a live listener stalling on a loopback 4-tuple still in TIME_WAIT,
# which is a property of the machine rather than of the oracle and has been
# seen taking longer than http_get's own ten-second bound.
eventually http_get $GPAO_STATUS/status

gpao stop
-- cluster --
validators: 3
code-submission-policy: inert
